l line. So we need two sunny lines for (2,1) and (2,2) separately, but we only have one left. So fails.\n\nThus seems impossible.\n\nThus k=2 seems impossible for n=4 as well.\n\nNow for n≥5, similar reasoning likely holds.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to verify the solution's reasoning for impossibility of k=2 and k≥4. The solution's argument for k≥4: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This reasoning seems a bit vague but likely valid. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have n columns (x=1,...,n). Each column i has h_i = n+1-i points. For i = n-2, h_{n-2}=3; for i=n-1, h_{n-1}=2; for i=n, h_n=1.\n\nIf k≥4, then there are at least 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines. For columns with many points (like column 1 has n points), we need many non-sunny lines to cover the rest. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines covering entire columns, or horizontal lines covering one row across many columns, or slope -1 lines covering diagonal.\n\nThe solution's argument: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This is not fully rigorous but seems plausible: we need at least one vertical line for each column that has more points than k (i.e., columns with h_i