uggests that k=2 is impossible because of constraints on covering the rightmost columns. Let's examine more systematically.\n\nWe need to cover all points in the triangular region with exactly n lines. The total number of points is N = n(n+1)/2. The average points per line is (n+1)/2. So we need to have some lines covering many points. The vertical lines x=1,...,x=n each cover a column of points: column x=i has points (i,b) where b ≤ n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points. So vertical lines cover decreasing numbers of points: column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1 points, ..., column n has 1 point. So using all vertical lines x=1,...,x=n gives n lines covering all points. That's the k=0 construction.\n\nNow, if we want to have some sunny lines, we need to replace some vertical lines with sunny lines. But sunny lines cannot be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. So they cannot be parallel to vertical lines. So they cannot cover a whole column. They can cover at most one point per column (since any two points in the same column have same x, so any line through them is vertical). So a sunny line can cover at most one point from each column. However, a sunny line can cover points from different columns, possibly many points, as long as they are not aligned vertically/horizontally/slope -1. So we need to see if we can replace some vertical lines with sunny lines while still covering all points with exactly n lines.\n\nThe solution's claim: \"If k≤2, we need a vertical line for column x=n-2.\" Let's examine column x=n-2 has 3 points. If we have at most 2 sunny lines, can we cover those 3 points without a vertical line? As argued, a sunny line can cover at most one point from that column. So with at most 2 sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points from that column. So we need a vertical line to cover the third point. So indeed we need at least one vertical line for column n-2.\n\nNow, after using a vertical line for column n-2, we have covered all 3 points in that column. Ac